# /etc/containers/systemd/vmsconsole.container — the console: the page and
# the API, its own process on this box (a system job on every server in М11,
# nothing in front). Its token writes the operator's rows only — vms/cameras/*,
# vms/next_id, vms/retention/*, vms/idem/* — and never placement; a camera
# created here is placed by the controller's next pass. The spool is mounted
# read-only (the console never records); the archive is writable because an
# operator's marks are the console's own buckets, console/<instance>/…, on it.
# ================================================================================================
# NOTES — what every part of this file does and why (kept beside the code, not in a separate document)
# ================================================================================================
# # vmsconsole.container — the console: the page and the API as its own process, with the operator's token and
# never placement
#
# **Role.** The Quadlet unit for `vmsconsole` at `/etc/containers/systemd/vmsconsole.container`. It runs
# `python3 -m vms console`: `SpecConsole` over the VMS spec with the VMS's two media routes, on
# `0.0.0.0:8080`. Its own process (a system job on every server in М11, nothing in front) with its own token —
# `vms/cameras/*`, `vms/next_id`, `vms/retention/*`, `vms/idem/*` (`SPEC.acl_console()`) — and never
# placement: a camera created here is placed by the controller's next pass. The header comment states the
# mounts' reasoning: the spool is read-only (the console never records); the archive is writable because an
# operator's marks are the console's own buckets, `console/<instance>/…`, on it — segments it only serves.
# Checked by `tests/test_deploy_units.py` (`/data/spool` ends with `:ro,z`).
#
# ## Stanza by stanza
#
# ### `[Unit]`
# - `Description=VMS console — the operator's rows, the read model, the page`
# - `After=network.target` — it listens on a port, so it waits for the network.
#
# ### `[Container]`
# - `Image=localhost/vmsserver:latest`
# - `Exec=python3 -m vms console` — the `console` verb: a `VmsController` opened as writer `vmsconsole` with
#   the console ACL, an `ArchiveResource($SPOOL, $ARCHIVE)`, and `vms.console.serve(...)`.
# - `Environment=CONSOLE_HOST=0.0.0.0` — listen on every interface (the code's default is `127.0.0.1`); with
#   `Network=host` this is the box's :8080.
# - `Environment=CONSOLE_PORT=8080` — the port the README's `PLATFORM_DIR=… python3 -m vms controller  # the
#   console on :8080` line refers to; the page, `/spec`, `/cameras`, `/where`, `/metrics`, `/marks`,
#   `/timeline`, `/segment` all live here.
# - `EnvironmentFile=/data/config/vms.env` — `PLATFORM_DIR`, `SPOOL`, `ARCHIVE`, `CAPACITY` (the fallback
#   capacity its controller object carries; it never places, so it is unused in practice), `LOG_LEVEL`.
# - `Volume=/data/platform:/data/platform:z` — read-write: the console's token writes camera rows, `next_id`,
#   derived retention rows and idempotency keys, and reads heartbeats for the read model.
# - `Volume=/data/spool:/data/spool:ro,z` — read-only. The console constructs an `ArchiveResource` with the
#   spool root (which `makedirs` it — already present on the box) but serves only from the archive; the mount
#   says it cannot record.
# - `Volume=/data/archive:/data/archive:z` — read-write, as the header says: operator marks are the console's
#   own event buckets, `console/<hostname:pid>/e1/…` on this server's resource (`POST /marks` →
#   `SpecConsole.mark`), and that is the one thing it writes there; segments are only read (`/segment/<path>`,
#   with `Range`). It never touches `vms/<cam>/…`, whose buckets have one writer, the worker holding the
#   epoch.
# - `Network=host` — the port is the box's own; no mapping.
#
# ### `[Service]`
# - `Restart=always` — the console holds nothing: idempotency keys are in the store (`vms/idem/*`), so a retry
#   that reaches the restarted instance (or another one) gets the same reply. Marks use epoch 1 forever
#   because `hostname:pid` names a fresh log per instance.
# - `RestartSec=2`.
#
# ### `[Install]`
# - `WantedBy=multi-user.target`.
#
# ## Notes
# - The split with the controller unit is the two ACLs cut from `vms/*` (see `vmsplatform/spec.py`): this
#   token cannot `place`, and `test_the_console_over_http` proves it with a `Forbidden`. Deleting a camera
#   here marks the row; the placement is taken back by `vmscontroller`'s next `unplace_deleted`.
# - "Count as many as you like" (`__main__.console` docstring): several consoles over one store are safe;
#   `test_a_retry_that_lands_on_another_console_is_one_camera`.
# ================================================================================================
[Unit]
Description=VMS console — the operator's rows, the read model, the page
After=network.target

[Container]
Image=localhost/vmsserver:latest
Exec=python3 -m vms console
Environment=CONSOLE_HOST=0.0.0.0
Environment=CONSOLE_PORT=8080
EnvironmentFile=/data/config/vms.env
Volume=/data/platform:/data/platform:z
Volume=/data/spool:/data/spool:ro,z
Volume=/data/archive:/data/archive:z
Network=host

[Service]
Restart=always
RestartSec=2

[Install]
WantedBy=multi-user.target
